# Search exclusions. Read by ripgrep (and therefore by every code search tool
# built on it) but NOT by git - everything listed here is still committed,
# still served to the browser, still imported at runtime. This file only
# decides what a search walks through.
#
# Why it exists: data/nba-players.js is 2,542 generated rows, and the fields it
# repeats on every one of them are ordinary identifiers. Searching the codebase
# for "team", "pos", "name" or "decade" - all of which appear in real app code -
# was pulling ~2,542 dataset lines into the results every single time, before
# getting to the handful of matches anyone wanted.
#
# CLAUDE.md already says not to read data/ while writing app code. This makes
# it true by default rather than by memory. Looking a player's numbers up is
# still perfectly possible - name the file directly, or pass --no-ignore - and
# having to do that deliberately is the point.
data/

# Build verification output: screenshots, videos and JSON from the browser
# harness. Already gitignored; listed again because .gitignore and .ignore are
# read by different tools and a search shouldn't wade through a run's
# artifacts either.
verify-artifacts/
verify-report.json
